# Mind maps — the map markdown dialect

`context/instructions/mindmap-markmap.md` is the contract for writing a mind map
as markdown, and for managing the resulting `.map.md` document in the project's
corpus. Read it together with `context/instructions/mindmap.md` (the
format-agnostic doctrine: the outline first, one keyword per node, the budgets)
and `context/instructions/wiki.md` (the relation discipline for corpus
documents). The doctrine file owns the budgets; this file owns the dialect
mechanics. The file keeps its historical name; the dialect is now the project's
own parser (a `marked` lexer walk plus the marker layer), not markmap.

## Nothing is dropped

Every block of the document becomes a node, so no content disappears silently:

| Block | Node |
|---|---|
| `h1..h6` | topic |
| `ul` / `ol` items (nested lists included) | topic |
| paragraph | topic (prose) |
| blockquote | quote, one line per quoted paragraph |
| fenced code block | code leaf (escaped, monospace) |
| table | table leaf (cells joined with ` · `, rows on separate lines) |
| anything else (raw HTML, a definition list) | text leaf holding its raw source |

Raw HTML is **escaped**, never rendered: a node label can carry emphasis, links,
`sup`/`sub` and images, but a document can never inject markup.

## Skeleton

- Exactly one root: a single `#` heading, or a frontmatter `title` as the root.
- `##` for main branches; deeper nesting through `###` headings and nested `-`
  lists.
- A list attaches to the **innermost open heading**. Do not mix a loose
  document-level list with sub-headings under one parent: the list lands under
  the deepest heading instead of where you meant it. Pick one level per parent.

## Markers

Markers are appended to a topic and abut its content (no space between the text
and the first marker). Several markers on one topic are allowed. `\[\]` is a
literal `[]`.

| Marker | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `[B]` / `[B<n>]` | boundary membership (id `0` when unnumbered); consecutive siblings with the same id are wrapped by one rounded rectangle |
| `[B<n>]: title` | names that boundary; the line is a declaration, never a topic |
| `[S]` / `[S<n>]`, `[S<n>]: title` | summary membership and title, wrapped like a boundary |
| `[n]` / `[^n]` | relationship source / target; the same number joins them. `[^n](Title)` gives the edge a title |
| `[N:text]` | note on the topic, plain text, repeatable |
| `[L:url]` | hyperlink on the topic |
| `[F]` | folded: the children start hidden and the topic carries a toggle |
| `[!name]` | SDT sticker — a small icon on the topic (see below) |
| `#group/<name>` | SDT cross-cutting group membership (see below) |

Markers are read from a **topic** — a heading, a list item or a paragraph — and
from nowhere else: inside a code block, a table cell or a blockquote they stay
literal text, and inside an inline code span or a link label they are still
read. Both implementations agree on that rule, so a marker never half-applies.

### Stickers (SDT extension)

XMindMark has no sticker syntax, so SDT spells it `[!name]` — `[n]` is taken by
relationships. The vocabulary is closed and renders as a Material Symbols icon:
`star`, `flag`, `idea`, `done`, `risk`, `question`, `goal`, `time`, `data`,
`priority`. An unknown name still renders as a chip, so a typo is visible rather
than silent.

### Group tags (overlay)

`#group/<name>` inside a node's text marks it as a member of a cross-cutting
group. This is a display convention, not parser syntax: a rendering tool strips
the tag from the label and draws one convex-hull boundary around every member,
including members that are not consecutive siblings — which the XMindMark `[B]`
rounded rectangles cannot wrap.

- The tag is a token **inside** a node, not a standalone heading: a line that is
  exactly `#group/x` parses as a level-1 heading.
- A node may carry several tags: `topic #group/auth #group/core`.
- The name is `[A-Za-z0-9][\w-]*`. The tag is node-scoped and unrelated to the
  document's `tags:` frontmatter.
- A group of fewer than three members falls back to its bounding rectangle.

## Inline formatting survives

Emphasis, `sup`/`sub`, links and images render inside a topic. A GFM task item
keeps its state as a literal `[ ]` / `[x]` prefix on the label.

## Frontmatter is not an options channel

There is no renderer options block: a `markmap:` key (and the old
`<!-- markmap: fold -->` comments) is inert, and the lint reports it. Folding is
`[F]`; the layout is a choice the rendering surface offers. Content never
belongs in frontmatter.

## Leaves

Tables, fenced code and blockquotes become leaves. Use them as leaves only;
never hide a branch inside one.

## Deviations from XMindMark

Deliberate, so the dialect does not drift toward a half-implemented copy of the
specification:

- **No summary sub-topics.** `[S<n>]: title` declares a title only.
- **No relationship on a title line.** XMindMark treats a boundary title as a
  topic and allows `[n]` on it; the marker layer ignores such a marker and the
  lint reports it.
- **Ids are document-global**, not scoped by indentation depth.
- **`[!name]` stickers are SDT's own** (see above).

## Parser scope

The map tree is built by `web/src/lib/mindmap.ts` (a `marked` lexer walk) and
annotated by `web/src/lib/mapMarkers.ts` (the marker layer). There is no
third-party parser to re-verify against a version: the re-verification duty is
the dialect's own test suite (`mindmap.test.ts`, `mapMarkers.test.ts`). Change a
rule here and change those tests with it — the lint in
`cli/cmd/context_maplint.go` mirrors this file, so the three move together.

## Document management (`.map.md`)

A map is an existing corpus document in a particular form, not a new kind:

- `.map.md` is a **suffix modifier on an existing document kind**, not a kind of
  its own.
- The document id is derived by stripping the `.md` extension only, so the map
  carries its **own** id (`context/notes/plan.map.md` → `context/notes/plan.map`)
  and links to its base document rather than aliasing it.
- Frontmatter and `kind` are inherited from the base document type; the map adds
  no kind-specific schema.
- **Typed relations** live in the `relations:` frontmatter map over the closed
  verb vocabulary: `supersedes`, `depends_on`, `refers_to`, `refines`,
  `implements`, `conflicts_with`, `part_of`, `contains`.
- `[[target|label]]` wikilinks, optionally with a `#anchor`, are ordinary
  navigation and are **not** graph edges on their own.
- **No duplication** — content lives in the map *or* in its base document, never
  in both. A map that copies its base creates a second source of truth that will
  drift: the map carries the shape, the base document keeps the prose.
